ifOpT -- SEQ 592 (Tony, 2026-11-10): If and ELSEIf as operators, WHILe's shape (SEQ 580). Both declare op="ifThen" and take
the default action OPERATOr; If's hand action (which ELSEIf shared) is deleted. The label's slots arrive as condition, then,
else, the order opIf reads, an absent else absent. ifT, elseIfT, branchT and ruleIfT's values must not move; these rows pin
what the default CHANGES for ELSEIf and the controls it must not. If's own three refusals are ruleIfT's RI-C / RI-S / RI-E
(both arms) and refusedCondT's RC-If / RC-sIf. Rows prefixed IO-. Interpreted only.

IO-1  ELSEIf's then slot refuses: today it says If (ELSEIf ran If's action); the default names ELSEIf and its slot.
IO-2  ELSEIf's else slot refuses: the same.
IO-3  an or arm refused inside an If: today two lines, both naming If (the or arm's then, then the If's else, the or arm
      having handed up nothing); the default names ELSEIf's slot, then If's ELSe.
IO-4  control: a bare-name condition is a value, not an instruct, and is not refused; run twice, true then false.
IO-5  control: the old road's If, a refused then arm in an action: refused naming xl1, the rest of the action does not run.
IO-6  control (R0): top level through FILe -- an if / or / else, then a plain if, then a print.
IO-7  control (R0): the same statements as a new-road action body (ioN, built by ioNew -- whileT's whNew). A "parse failed"
      line would mean ioN ran on the old road; the refusals column would show it.
IO-8  control: a bare name as the else statement builds, and the false condition runs it, printing nothing.

Not run alone: pop.sh appends the row, print "NEXT", a sentinel; one process per row. Each row reads exit and sentinel; stdout
(lines joined by |); and the lines that are REFUSED naming If, ELSEIf, ifArms or xl1, or say parse failed, joined by |.

ROWS, separated by ^ -- id ^ statements ^ stdout today ^ refusals today ^ stdout intended ^ refusals intended
